// Refusal Contracts — a team's security policy, enforced by the compiler.
//
// Heat ships with the GDPR / PCI / HIPAA / SOX refusal lattice built in
// (@pii, @cardholder_data, ... can't reach an audit sink). But every
// service has data categories the regulators never named: internal
// customer IDs, partner API keys, model prompts. A Refusal Contract lets
// a team add ITS OWN rule and have the compiler enforce it exactly like a
// built-in one — the policy moves out of the wiki and into the language.
//
// Here: privacy review SEC-412 ruled that internal customer IDs must
// never appear verbatim in the audit log. We declare that as an
// obligation. The broken call below trips it.
//
// ❌ Expected: error[NL-OBL-001] — does not produce a binary.

// A project-specific sensitive data category. Not regulated PII — the
// built-in lattice knows nothing about it. The team defines what it means.
tag customer_id:
    "Internal customer identifiers in this service. Privacy review SEC-412
     ruled these must never appear verbatim in the audit log."

// The obligation IS the policy. `forbid @SRC -> @SINK` is checked at every
// call site; the docstring and repair line are quoted back in the error.
obligation NoCustomerIdInAuditLog:
    "Internal customer IDs must never reach the audit log verbatim."
    repair "convert to a pseudonymous token via audit_token(raw:) first"
    forbid @customer_id -> @audit_safe

// The audit-log sink: its @audit_safe parameter is the disclosure
// boundary. Only values cleared as @audit_safe may cross it.
fn audit_log(msg: String @audit_safe) -> Int [io]:
    print(value: format("[audit] {m}", m: msg))
    return 0

// The source: reading a customer record stamps the id @customer_id.
fn read_customer_id() -> String @customer_id [io]:
    return "cust_8842"

fn main() -> Int [io]:
    let cid = read_customer_id()
    // ❌ NL-OBL-001: cid carries @customer_id; audit_log needs @audit_safe.
    // The obligation refuses the flow and cites SEC-412's repair.
    return audit_log(msg: cid)
